{
  "dom_id": "step-q47534",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14273993/assets/543d468d15faa102ed0813c3e1d3b4e490c8bb8617334c55ff5dd7d3119bba05.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Approximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=4$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=1.$",
  "worked_solution": "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\nat the point $x=4$ with step size $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(4) & ≈\\frac{f(4+1)-f(4-1)}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(5)-f(3)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-0}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
